= Six Gentlemen Decoction with Aucklandia and Amomum (Xiāng Shā Liù Jūnzǐ Tāng, 香砂六君子湯)

_Six Gentlemen plus *木香* and *砂仁*: the Spleen-qi tonic extended a second time, now to handle the *qi stagnation* that makes a vacuity patient's epigastrium frankly painful rather than merely full. It is a second-order derivative — 四君子湯 → 六君子湯 → this — so it restates neither parent; see four-gentlemen-decoction.typ for the 補中有瀉 architecture and six-gentlemen-decoction.typ for the phlegm extension. Three things make it worth its own treatment. First, it is the point in the family chain at which the formula *stops being predominantly a tonic*: eight named drugs, of which three are aromatic qi-movers, and the arithmetic of supplementation against movement is no longer lopsided. Second, it is the standard classical answer to *虛不受補* — the vacuity so deep that the patient cannot digest the tonic — which is the clinical situation the whole chain exists to reach. Third, and least comfortable: it is routinely treated as interchangeable with its parent, and it is not. 六君子湯 has a randomized-trial literature under its Kampo name; this formula, with two further actives and no equivalent standardized product, does not, and the evidence does not transfer._

== Name & Meaning

*香砂六君子湯* — *香* + *砂* + *六君子湯*. The name follows the ordinary Chinese convention for naming a derivative: *abbreviate each added drug to one character and prefix it to the parent's name*. *香* stands for *木香 (mùxiāng)* and *砂* for *砂仁 (shārén)*, so the whole reads "Six Gentlemen Decoction with Aucklandia and Amomum."

*The two added drug-names, unpacked:*
- *木香* — literally "wood aroma" / "aromatic wood," from the dense, strongly scented root; the drug is not a wood, and the name records the character of its smell rather than its botany.
- *砂仁* — literally "sand kernel," *砂* (sand, grit) + *仁* (kernel, seed), from the granular mass of small seeds inside the fruit. The older classical drug-name is *縮砂蜜 / 縮砂仁 (suōshāmì / suōshārén)*, which is the form the Song sources use and which survives in the Hejijufang's 參苓白朮散 ingredient list — see `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`.

*A real ambiguity in the name, and it is not pedantry.* The character *香* is not uniquely determined by the abbreviation: it can equally stand for *香附 (xiāngfù,* cyperus) or *藿香 (huòxiāng,* agastache), and *transmitted versions of this formula exist that substitute one of those for 木香*. The standard reading — and the composition given below — is *木香*; but a prescription or a patent labelled 香砂 should be read for its actual ingredient list rather than inferred from its name. *Flagged for verification against a specific edition* rather than smoothed over.

*Standard English renderings:* "Six-Gentleman Decoction with Aucklandia and Amomum" (Bensky's rendering, and the one this entry's slug follows), "Costus and Amomum Combination with Six Noble Ingredients," and in the patent trade simply "Xiang Sha Liu Jun Zi" or "Saussurea and Cardamom Combination." *Japanese:* 香砂六君子湯 *Kōsha rikkunshitō* — but note that *this is not one of the standardized Kampo preparations* and carries no TJ product number, unlike its parent 六君子湯 (TJ-43). *Korean:* 향사육군자탕 *hyangsa yukgunjatang*.

*The counting quirk is inherited and compounded.* The parent is called "six" and usually holds eight, because *生薑* and *大棗* are the standard *薑棗* pair and are conventionally not counted (see `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`). This formula adds two *named* drugs to the six, so the roster is *eight named drugs and ten items in the pot* — and the name still says "six."

*Look-alike names, which are a practical hazard at the counter rather than an etymological curiosity.* Several widely sold preparations share the *香砂* prefix and are *different formulas*:
- *香砂六君丸 (Xiāng Shā Liù Jūn Wán)* — the pill form of *this* formula, and the one actually meant most of the time.
- *香砂養胃丸 / 湯 (Xiāng Shā Yǎng Wèi)* — for cold-damp obstructing the Stomach; built on *蒼朮, 厚朴, 香附* rather than on the Gentlemen core, markedly more drying and much less tonifying.
- *香砂枳術丸 (Xiāng Shā Zhǐ Zhú Wán)* — a 消導 (dispersing-and-conducting) formula on 枳實 + 白朮, for food accumulation with Spleen vacuity.
- *香砂平胃散* — 平胃散 with the aromatics; damp without vacuity.
*Only the first is this formula.* The others answer adjacent but distinct patterns, and the shared prefix names only the shared aromatic pair.#footnote[The one-character-per-added-drug naming convention is general in Chinese formula nomenclature (cf. 柴芍六君子湯, 桃紅四物湯). 木香 and 砂仁 etymologies follow the standard drug-name glosses; 縮砂蜜 as the older name is the form used in the Song sources. The ambiguity of 香 across 木香 / 香附 / 藿香 in 香砂-prefixed formula names is a recognized source of confusion in the formula literature and is flagged here rather than resolved. English renderings: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; patent-trade names vary by manufacturer. The absence of a Kampo TJ designation for this formula, in contrast to 六君子湯, is consequential for the evidence base and is taken up under Modern Pharmacology.]

== Source & History

*The attribution is variable, more so than for the parent, and should be stated as such.* Modern formula texts most commonly credit the formula to *羅美 (Luo Mei)'s 古今名醫方論 (Gǔjīn Míngyī Fānglùn,* "Discussions of Formulas by Famous Physicians Past and Present," 1675). A formula of this name also appears in *龔廷賢 (Gong Tingxian)'s 萬病回春 (Wànbìng Huíchūn,* "Restoring Spring to Myriad Diseases," 1587), and the family is discussed in *汪昂 (Wang Ang)'s 醫方集解 (Yīfāng Jíjiě,* 1682). *What is secure* is that the formula is a *late-Ming to early-Qing formalization* of a modification already obvious to anyone holding 六君子湯 and the aromatic qi-regulators — not a single-author invention, and not a Song formula. *Which text to treat as the source, and what composition that text actually gives, should be verified against a specific edition* rather than taken from this entry.

*Its position in the family.* The Four Gentlemen lineage grows by addition, each step answering a problem the previous one leaves open:
- *四君子湯* — tonifies Spleen qi. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *異功散* — *+ 陳皮*; qi-regulation, so that supplementation does not stagnate.
- *六君子湯* — *+ 陳皮 + 半夏*; phlegm-transformation, because Spleen vacuity generates damp which congeals to phlegm. See `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *香砂六君子湯* — *+ 木香 + 砂仁*; aromatic qi-moving and middle-warming, for *frank pain and distension*. The most-used derivative, and effectively the *terminus* of the addition chain — see the architectural note below on why the chain stops here.
- *參苓白朮散* branches off separately, for damp with chronic diarrhoea below. See `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`.

*In modern practice* the pill form *香砂六君丸* is a Chinese Pharmacopoeia patent and among the most widely sold over-the-counter preparations for dyspepsia, bloating and poor appetite in China; the decoction is in constant clinical use in gastroenterology. *Unlike the parent it did not enter the Japanese standardized-extract system*, which is the single most important fact about its evidence base.#footnote[Attribution to 羅美, _Gǔjīn Míngyī Fānglùn_ (古今名醫方論, 1675) is the most common in modern formula texts — Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; a formula of the same name in 龔廷賢's _Wànbìng Huíchūn_ (萬病回春, 1587) and the family discussion in 汪昂's _Yīfāng Jíjiě_ (醫方集解, 1682) are the usual competing or complementary citations. The formula is best understood as a late-Ming/early-Qing formalization of an obvious modification; flagged for verification against a specific edition rather than asserted. 香砂六君丸 as a Pharmacopoeia patent: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_, current edition.]

== Composition

*Classical proportions.* Sources differ, and the differences are not trivial — some versions weight the aromatics at a third of the tonics, some at half. The common modern rendering gives the four Gentlemen in roughly equal parts, 陳皮 and 半夏 at comparable or slightly lower weight, and *木香 and 砂仁 lower still*, plus the 薑棗 pair. *The low weighting of the two additions is part of the formula, not a rounding convention*: at tonic-level doses they would overturn the prescription's balance, which is the point taken up under Architecture.

*Modern clinical decoction doses* (reference figures requiring verification against current pharmacopoeial and clinical practice): 人參 6–9 g (or 黨參 12–15 g); 白朮 9–12 g; 茯苓 9–12 g; 炙甘草 3–6 g; 陳皮 6–9 g; *半夏 (processed)* 6–9 g; *木香 3–6 g* (added late); *砂仁 3–6 g* (*後下*); 生薑 3–6 g; 大棗 3–5 pieces.

*Four processing and handling requirements that are not optional:*
- *半夏 must be a processed form* — 清半夏, 姜半夏 or 法半夏. *生半夏 (raw) is toxic and is never given in decoction.* See `substances/ban-xia.typ`.
- *炙甘草* (honey-fried), not raw 生甘草, which is cooler and clears heat.
- *砂仁 is added 後下*, in the last few minutes; its action resides in a volatile oil.
- *木香 is likewise not boiled long* — *木香不宜久煎* — for the same reason. Where diarrhoea rather than pain dominates, the roasted form *煨木香* is substituted; this is a choice of *drug-form*, not of dose.

*A note on role assignment, because it varies here more than in the parents.* This entry keeps the sibling entries' assignment for the Gentlemen core (人參 君, 白朮 臣, 茯苓 佐, 炙甘草 使) for continuity. Many commentators, reading the formula as *two* formulas in one, instead promote 陳皮 and 半夏 to 臣 and place 木香 and 砂仁 as 佐 — and some treat 人參 and 白朮 jointly as 君. *Role assignment is interpretive and commentators differ*; see *君臣佐使* in `zz-glossary.typ`. Nothing clinical hangs on the choice.

=== 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 炙甘草 (君臣 — the Four Gentlemen core)

_Treated as one block, since its internal architecture is the subject of the base entry and restating it here would duplicate. Each drug still carries its property line, per this book's self-contained rule._

- *人參 (rénshēn,* ginseng) — *君*: sweet, slightly bitter; slightly warm (微溫); Spleen, Lung, Heart, Kidney. *大補元氣*, *補脾益肺*, *生津*, *安神益智*. See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.
- *白朮 (báizhú,* atractylodes rhizome) — *臣*: bitter, sweet; warm (溫); Spleen, Stomach. *健脾益氣*, *燥濕利水*, *止汗*, *安胎*. Usually *炒白朮*. *No substance entry yet.*
- *茯苓 (fúlíng,* poria) — *佐*: sweet, bland (淡); neutral (平); Spleen, Heart, Kidney. *利水滲濕*, *健脾*, *寧心安神*. See `substances/fuling.typ`.
- *炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo,* honey-fried licorice) — *使*: sweet; warm (溫) in the honey-fried form; Heart, Lung, Spleen, Stomach. *補脾益氣*, *調和諸藥*, *緩急止痛*. See `substances/chinese-licorice.typ`.

*Function in this formula:* the tonify-and-drain core, unchanged in composition but changed again in job description. In 四君子湯 the quartet is the whole prescription; in 六君子湯 it is the half that addresses the cause while two drugs address the phlegm; *here it is the part of the formula that is still doing supplementation at all*, against four drugs that move, dry or drain. *One of its members takes on extra significance*: 炙甘草's *緩急止痛* (relaxes urgency, stops pain) is a minor note in the parents and a relevant action here, since pain is the presenting complaint.

=== 半夏 (bànxià, prepared pinellia) — 臣, minister

*Properties:* Acrid/pungent (辛); warm (溫); *有毒* (toxic — a formal property, hence the processing requirement). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *燥濕化痰* (dries damp and transforms phlegm — the category's chief drug), *降逆止嘔* (directs counterflow downward, stops vomiting), *消痞散結* (disperses glomus and dissolves accumulation).

*Function in this formula:* as in the parent, it dries and transforms the phlegm that Spleen vacuity generates, and supplies the *降逆止嘔* action that gives the family's upward-directed symptoms — nausea, vomiting — their remedy. *What is worth noticing here is the third action, 消痞散結*, which is a minor note in 六君子湯 and pulls its weight in this formula: *痞* is the stuffy, blocked, immovable sensation under the sternum, and dispersing it is continuous with what 木香 and 砂仁 are added to do. *The formula's three "unblocking" drugs are therefore 半夏, 木香 and 砂仁 working on the same obstruction from three directions* — dissolving it, pushing it through, and rousing the organ that should be moving it.

*Which form matters:* 姜半夏 (ginger-and-alum processed) inclines toward the antiemetic action, the apt choice where vomiting dominates; 清半夏 toward phlegm-damp. *生半夏 is never used.* See `substances/ban-xia.typ` for the five processed drug-forms, the raw drug's toxicity, and the 十八反 incompatibility.

=== 陳皮 (chénpí, aged tangerine peel) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Pungent (辛), bitter (苦), *aromatic* (芳香); warm (溫). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *理氣健脾* (regulates qi, strengthens the Spleen), *燥濕化痰* (dries damp, transforms phlegm).

*Function in this formula — and here it is the mildest of three qi-movers rather than the only one, which changes how it should be read.* Its job remains what it is in 異功散 and 六君子湯: it *moves qi*, keeping sweet tonics from becoming cloying, and it *assists 半夏 in transforming phlegm* by a complementary route — where 半夏 dries and descends, 陳皮 moves and disperses. *Its range is the chest and the upper middle*, and its qi-moving is gentle and diffuse. That gentleness is exactly why it is insufficient for the pattern this formula treats, and why two stronger aromatics are added rather than simply raising its dose: *陳皮 at double dose disperses more in the same place*, which is not the same as reaching the lower abdomen and the bowel.

*The aged form is specified* — 陳皮 means "aged peel," conventionally three years or more, the harsher volatile fraction having mellowed. See `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`.

=== 木香 (mùxiāng, aucklandia root) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Pungent/acrid (辛), bitter (苦), *strongly aromatic* (芳香); warm (溫). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Large Intestine (大腸), Triple Burner (三焦), Gallbladder (膽). *Principal actions:* *行氣止痛* (moves qi and stops pain — the drug's signature and the reason it is here), *健脾消食* (strengthens the Spleen and disperses food stagnation), *疏肝利膽* (courses the Liver and benefits the Gallbladder), *調中導滯* (regulates the middle and conducts away stagnation).

*Function in this formula — it is the drug that converts a formula for fullness into a formula for pain.* The distinction matters because the tradition treats them as different things: *痞悶* is stuffy oppression, a sensation of blockage; *脹痛* is distension that actually hurts, and it indicates qi stagnation that has become fixed rather than merely sluggish. *行氣止痛 is the action for the second*, and no other drug in the parent formula has it at any strength.

*Why this drug rather than a near-neighbour, and the answer is in the channel list.* 木香 is the only ingredient here entering the *Large Intestine* and the *Triple Burner*, and that reach is what the formula gains: distension with borborygmus, bowel discomfort and incomplete evacuation lies below 陳皮's range and inside 木香's. Its *Gallbladder* and Liver entry adds a second useful property — where the vacuity pattern is complicated by genuine Liver constraint, 木香 gives the formula a small purchase on it, which is why the 柴芍 modification below is a short step rather than a different prescription. Compared with *香附*, the other candidate for the *香* slot, 木香 is the better *pain-and-bowel* drug while 香附 is the better *Liver-and-menstrual* drug; compared with *枳實*, it moves qi without 枳實's downward-breaking force, which a vacuity patient does not need.

*Drug-forms, and the distinction is clinically used:* *生木香* (raw) for 行氣止痛, the default here; *煨木香* (roasted in ashes or bran-roasted) loses some of the volatile oil and inclines toward *止瀉*, checking diarrhoea — the form to use where loose stools rather than pain dominate.

*Its volatility is a preparation constraint*: *木香不宜久煎*, the drug should not be boiled long. See Preparation.

*No substance entry yet* — and note the identity hazard recorded under Cautions, which is the most serious safety point in this formula: *青木香* is a different plant, an _Aristolochia_, and is nephrotoxic.

=== 砂仁 (shārén, amomum fruit) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Acrid/pungent (辛); warm (溫); *aromatic* (芳香). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃) — the attribution used elsewhere in this book; the current *Chinese Pharmacopoeia* adds *Kidney (腎)*, which is what underwrites the 安胎 and middle-warming claims. *Principal actions:* *化濕行氣* (transforms damp and moves qi), *醒脾開胃* (rouses the Spleen and opens the appetite), *溫中止瀉* (warms the middle and checks diarrhoea), *安胎* (calms the fetus).

*Function in this formula — and it is not a duplicate of 木香, which is the usual misreading of the pair.* The two additions divide the work:
- *木香* addresses *stagnation that hurts* — it unblocks and pushes through. Its register is *pain*.
- *砂仁* addresses *an organ too torpid to move anything* — it *rouses* (醒脾), by the *芳香化濕* mechanism in which volatile aromatics stimulate a damp-encumbered Spleen rather than attacking the damp directly. Its register is *appetite and damp*.

*So one drug clears the road and the other starts the engine*, and a patient with distension but intact appetite needs mostly the first while a patient with no appetite at all needs mostly the second. 砂仁 also *warms the middle*, which matters because this formula is routinely given where the damp has a cold character.

*Why this drug:* 砂仁 is the *type drug* of 芳香化濕 and the one the tradition reaches for first in the category; *白豆蔻* is the nearer substitute, lighter and more upper-middle in range, and *藿香* is the choice where summer-damp or nausea with exterior involvement is in play.

*Its volatility is a preparation constraint, not a detail*: the active fraction is the essential oil and prolonged boiling drives it off, so 砂仁 is added *後下* (late). See Preparation.

*No substance entry yet*; it is glossed in `zz-glossary.typ` and treated within `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`, where it performs the same 芳香化濕 role in a differently-aimed formula. Its classical pairing with *熟地黃* to correct that drug's 滋膩 cloying property is recorded in `substances/rehmannia.typ` and `formulas/four-substances-decoction.typ` — the same function, applied to a blood tonic instead of a qi tonic.

=== 生薑, 大棗 (shēngjiāng, dàzǎo — the 薑棗 pair) — 佐使, assistant and envoy

- *生薑 (fresh ginger):* Pungent (辛); warm (溫); Lung, Spleen, Stomach. *發散風寒*, *溫中止嘔* (classically 嘔家聖藥, "the holy medicine of the vomiting patient"), *化痰止咳*, and *解半夏天南星毒* — *it detoxifies pinellia*. See `substances/ginger.typ`.
- *大棗 (jujube):* Sweet (甘), intensely so; warm (溫); Spleen, Stomach, secondarily Heart and Lung. *補中益氣*, *養血安神*, *緩和藥性* (moderates the harshness of other drugs). See `substances/jujube-dates.typ`.

*Function in this formula — and 大棗 is doing more here than in the parent.* 生薑 serves its three usual purposes: reinforcing the antiemetic action, warming the middle, and standing as 半夏's classical corrective, so that the formula contains both the toxic drug and its antidote. *大棗's buffering role is proportionally larger*, because the thing being buffered has grown: the parent had two acrid-drying drugs to offset, this formula has *four* (半夏, 陳皮, 木香, 砂仁) plus 白朮's drying and 茯苓's draining. Its sweetness and moistening are what keep a formula of that composition from desiccating a depleted patient. See *薑棗* in `zz-glossary.typ`.

== Formula Architecture

_For the tonify-and-drain core see `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`, and for the phlegm extension see `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`. What follows is what the second extension accomplishes, why the chain stops here, and the clinical situation the whole structure exists to reach._

*Three qi-movers, not one repeated three times.* The commonest misreading of this formula is that 陳皮, 木香 and 砂仁 are redundant — three warm aromatic qi-regulators where one would do. They are not, and the division is as clean as the three damp mechanisms of 參苓白朮散:
- *陳皮* — *disperses* in the chest and upper middle, and transforms phlegm. Gentle, diffuse, broad.
- *木香* — *unblocks and conducts through*, reaching the Large Intestine and Triple Burner. Its target is *pain*.
- *砂仁* — *rouses* a torpid Spleen aromatically, and warms. Its target is *appetite and damp*.
*A chronic case commonly needs all three*, for the same reason a chronic damp case needs drying, leaching and aromatic transformation at once: the obstruction has more than one character, and a drug that addresses one of them leaves the others standing.

*The arithmetic, which is the formula's real structural interest.* Count the roster by what each drug does:
- *Supplementing:* 人參, 白朮, 炙甘草, 大棗 — four.
- *Moving, drying, draining or descending:* 茯苓, 半夏, 陳皮, 木香, 砂仁, 生薑 — six.

*This is 補中有瀉 pushed to — and arguably past — its limit.* In 四君子湯 three tonics stand against one drainer and the formula is unambiguously a tonic with a corrective. Here the proportions are inverted on the count, and only the *dosing* keeps it a tonic: the Gentlemen core carries the weight in grams while the aromatics are deliberately held low. *Change the doses and you change the formula's category*, which is why the low weighting of 木香 and 砂仁 noted under Composition is compositional rather than incidental. *This observation is this book's own framing of the structure*, not a classical formulation — but it has a classical consequence, which is the next point.

*Why the addition chain stops here.* 異功散, 六君子湯 and 香砂六君子湯 each add what the previous formula lacked, and the obvious question is why the series does not continue. *The answer is that it has run out of room.* Qi-moving drugs are *耗氣* — they consume qi in the act of moving it — and a formula built to supplement qi can only carry so many of them before it is working against itself. At eight named drugs with three aromatics, further additions do not extend the formula; they convert it into something else. *This is why the named relatives listed below (香砂枳術丸, 香砂養胃丸, 香砂平胃散) are siblings rather than descendants*: each keeps the aromatic pair and *drops or demotes the tonic core*, which is the only direction left.

*The clinical situation the chain exists to reach: 虛不受補.* The phrase — "vacuity that cannot receive supplementation" — names the paradox that the patients who most need tonifying are often the ones least able to digest a tonic. A Spleen weak enough to fail at transforming food will also fail at transforming a decoction of sweet, heavy tonic drugs, and the prescription then sits in the middle burner producing exactly the distension, appetite loss and greasy coat it was given to relieve. *This formula is one of the standard classical answers to that problem*: it makes the tonic *transportable* by surrounding it with drugs that move, rouse and unblock, so that supplementation can be delivered to an organ that could not otherwise receive it. The same logic, applied to blood tonics instead of qi tonics, produces the 砂仁-and-陳皮 correction of 熟地黃's 滋膩 property recorded in `formulas/four-substances-decoction.typ` — *the two cases are the same manoeuvre on different material*.

*The formula's direction gains a third vector.* 四君子湯 is a formula without a strong directional character; 六君子湯 is *upward*-concerned, answering counterflow (nausea, vomiting, sputum); 參苓白朮散 is *downward*-concerned, answering diarrhoea. *This formula adds a through-and-outward vector* — 通, unblocking — by way of 木香's Large Intestine and Triple Burner reach and 半夏's 消痞散結. That is what lets it handle the horizontal complaints the others cannot: distension across the abdomen, borborygmus, bowel discomfort, incomplete evacuation.

*What breaks if an ingredient is removed:*
- *Remove 木香* and the formula reverts to something near 六君子湯: competent for nausea, sputum and fullness, inadequate for *pain*, and with no reach below the epigastrium.
- *Remove 砂仁* and the damp is dried and leached but the Spleen is never roused; appetite in particular fails to recover, and the formula loses its warming correction for cold-damp.
- *Remove both* and it *is* 六君子湯.
- *Remove 半夏* and the result is the 香砂異功散 register — qi-moving on a tonic base with no phlegm action, and nothing addressing 痞.
- *Remove 大棗* and four acrid-drying drugs lose their buffer in a patient who is already depleted.
- *Remove the Gentlemen core* and what remains is a plain aromatic qi-regulating formula of the 香砂平胃散 or 厚朴溫中湯 type — appropriate for stagnation in a robust patient, and exactly wrong for the one this formula is for.#footnote[The three-qi-mover division of labour, the 耗氣 liability of qi-regulating drugs in a tonic formula, and the 虛不受補 problem are all classical commonplaces in the formula-theory literature; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., and standard modern teaching texts. The supplementing-versus-moving count, the reading of the family chain as terminating for structural reasons, and the parallel drawn with the 砂仁 correction of 熟地黃 in 四物湯 are this book's own organization and framing of standard material, and are flagged as such. The "remove an ingredient" analysis is the conventional pedagogical treatment. For 芳香化濕 and the three damp mechanisms see `zz-glossary.typ` and `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`.]

== Indications & Pattern

*Pattern:* *脾胃氣虛，濕阻氣滯* — Spleen-Stomach qi vacuity with damp obstruction and qi stagnation; frequently with a *cold* character to the damp (寒濕), which is why the formula is warm throughout.

*Presenting signs:*
- *脘腹脹痛* — epigastric and abdominal distension *with pain*. *This is the decisive sign*, and the one that separates the formula from its parent: fullness alone is 六君子湯 territory, fullness that hurts is this formula's.
- *噯氣* — belching; *噫氣不舒*, belching that does not relieve.
- *納呆, 食少* — no interest in food, early satiety; appetite characteristically worse than the parent pattern's.
- *噁心嘔吐* — nausea and vomiting, inherited from the parent pattern.
- *腸鳴* — borborygmus; *大便溏薄* or *不爽* — loose stools, or evacuation that feels incomplete.
- *倦怠乏力*, *面色萎白* — fatigue, weak limbs, a pale sallow complexion: the vacuity floor, which must be present.
- *Tongue:* pale (舌淡) with a *white greasy coat* (苔白膩). *Pulse:* weak (虛弱), often soggy (濡) or slow-moderate (緩).

*The diagnostic requirement is all three of: vacuity signs, a white greasy coat, and frank distension-with-pain.* Drop the pain and the prescription is 六君子湯; drop the vacuity signs and it is 平胃散 or 香砂平胃散; drop the greasy coat and it is a different problem altogether. *A fourth, negative requirement:* the pain must be *dull, distending and better for warmth and pressure* — the vacuity-cold character. Pain that is fixed, stabbing and worse for pressure is not this pattern (see the stasis look-alike below).

*Modern correlates — correlations, not equivalences:* functional dyspepsia, particularly *postprandial distress syndrome* with bloating and early satiety; chronic superficial and atrophic gastritis; irritable bowel syndrome with bloating predominant; diabetic gastroparesis; post-operative ileus and slow return of appetite; chemotherapy-associated anorexia and abdominal distension; chronic cholecystitis as an adjunct. *See the modern traps below, of which this formula has two that matter.*

*Differentiation* — once the pattern is *correctly* identified, these are the neighbouring prescriptions:
- *六君子湯* — the same vacuity and phlegm-damp *without* frank pain and distension. See `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *四君子湯* — vacuity alone, no damp, no stagnation.
- *異功散* — vacuity with mild fullness; the smallest member of the chain to contain a qi-mover.
- *參苓白朮散* — the same vacuity and damp directed *downward*, where chronic diarrhoea dominates. See `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`.
- *香砂枳術丸* — food accumulation (食積) with Spleen vacuity; 枳實 and 白朮 rather than the Gentlemen core, dispersing rather than tonifying.
- *香砂養胃丸 / 湯* — cold-damp obstructing the Stomach, in a patient with less marked vacuity; drier (蒼朮, 厚朴) and far less tonifying.
- *平胃散* — damp obstruction *without* vacuity: a robust patient, thick greasy coat, no fatigue, no weak pulse.
- *厚朴溫中湯* — cold-damp with distension and pain, again *without* the vacuity floor.
- *理中丸* — frank Spleen-Stomach vacuity *cold*: cold limbs, watery diarrhoea, pain clearly relieved by warmth, and no greasy coat.
- *保和丸* — food stagnation: aversion to food, sour or rotten belching, thick coat.
- *柴胡疏肝散 / 四逆散 / 逍遙散* — Liver constraint as the *primary* pattern. The most important differentiation, and treated at length below because it is also the commonest diagnostic error.
- *柴芍六君子湯* — where Spleen vacuity and Liver constraint are *both* genuinely present, which is not rare.

== Misapplication & Look-Alike Patterns

_This formula's errors follow from one fact about its composition: it is the member of the family with the *most* warm, acrid, drying, qi-moving material in it. Everything 六君子湯 does wrong, it does more of — and it adds one failure mode of its own, which is harming a patient by moving qi that did not need moving. Consequences below are clinical commonplaces except where flagged._

=== If given to the wrong pattern

- *Stomach or Spleen yin vacuity (胃陰虛 / 脾陰虛) — the characteristic error, and worse here than in the parent.* *Mechanism:* the formula now contains *four* acrid-warm-drying drugs (半夏, 陳皮, 木香, 砂仁) plus 白朮's drying and 茯苓's draining, in a patient whose deficit is *fluid*, not qi. 木香 in particular is classically described as *辛溫香燥* and cautioned in yin vacuity. *Consequence:* worsened dry mouth and throat, *dry retching* rather than productive nausea, burning rather than distending epigastric discomfort, constipation with dry stool, and a tongue growing redder and barer — and *appearing faster than with 六君子湯*, often within days. *Correction:* a moistening formula (益胃湯, 麥門冬湯 register); the nuance recorded in `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ` — that 麥門冬湯 itself carries a small dose of 半夏 — applies to 半夏 and *not* to 木香, which has no analogous place in a moistening prescription.
- *Qi vacuity without stagnation — the error specific to this formula, and the one the family chain invites.* *Mechanism:* qi-moving drugs are *耗氣*, they consume qi in the act of moving it. Given to a patient with pure 氣虛 and no distension, the three aromatics scatter what the four tonics are supplying, and the formula partly cancels itself. *Consequence:* not dramatic harm but *failure plus deterioration* — fatigue worse rather than better after one to two weeks, appetite flat, sometimes increased sweating and shortness of breath. *Correction:* step back down the chain — 六君子湯, 異功散 or 四君子湯 — which is counterintuitive, because the instinct when a patient is very depleted is to reach for the *largest* member of the family. *The chain is not ordered by severity of vacuity; it is ordered by what accompanies the vacuity.* The size of the formula should track the complexity of the picture, not the depth of the deficiency. *The mechanism is classical; the framing of this as the chain's characteristic prescribing error is this book's inference.*
- *Phlegm-heat or Stomach heat (痰熱 / 胃熱).* *Mechanism:* every active drug in the formula is warm; heat added to heat, now with two more warm drugs than the parent. *Consequence:* sputum turning yellow and sticky, bitter taste, burning epigastric pain, acid reflux worsening, vexation and insomnia, a yellowing greasy coat. *Correction:* 溫膽湯 or the 清氣化痰丸 register; where acid reflux dominates, 左金丸.
- *Liver constraint transforming to fire (肝鬱化火).* *Mechanism:* a mixed case, and the most insidious. The qi-moving half of the formula is *right* for the constraint, which is why the patient may improve briefly; but the formula is warm and tonifying, which is wrong for the fire. *Consequence:* initial relief of distension followed by worsening heartburn, bitter taste, irritability and insomnia — a pattern of improvement-then-deterioration that is easily misread as the formula "needing more time." *Correction:* cool and course the Liver first (丹梔逍遙散, 左金丸 register), tonify the Spleen afterwards.
- *Excess damp or food accumulation without vacuity.* *Mechanism:* four supplementing drugs added to a middle burner that is full rather than empty — *補其實*, supplementing what is already full. *Consequence:* increased fullness, appetite worsening rather than improving, thickening coat. *Correction:* 平胃散, 香砂平胃散 or 保和丸 alone; tonify later if vacuity emerges.
- *Acute exterior illness.* The standing rule against tonifying while a pathogen is unresolved — *閉門留寇*, "shutting the door and leaving the bandit inside." *Consequence:* a protracted illness. *Correction:* release the exterior first.
- *Two dispensing errors rather than pattern errors, and they belong here because both are injuries rather than failures:*
  - *生半夏* (raw pinellia) supplied in place of a processed form produces acute oropharyngeal burning, numbness and swelling *on the first dose*. Always specify the processed form. See `substances/ban-xia.typ`.
  - *青木香 (qīng mùxiāng) supplied as 木香* — and this is the most serious safety point in the entry. 青木香 is the root of *_Aristolochia debilis_*, a *different plant in a different family*, and it contains *aristolochic acid*: nephrotoxic, causing irreversible interstitial fibrosis, and a recognized urothelial carcinogen with a latency of years. *The injury is latent, so there is no presenting consequence to notice* — which is precisely what makes name-confusion dangerous here rather than merely sloppy. *Correction:* specify *木香 (Aucklandia)* explicitly and reject 青木香 outright; see Cautions for the full set of name-alike drugs.

=== Patterns mistaken for this one

- *Liver qi invading the Stomach and Spleen (肝胃不和 / 肝脾不調) — the commonest error by a wide margin.* *Why it resembles:* distension, pain, belching and poor appetite are the shared presenting signs, and they are *the formula's own leading indications*. *Distinguishing signs, and there are three:* (a) *location* — rib-side and hypochondriac (脅) distension rather than centred on the epigastrium; (b) *pulse* — *wiry* (弦) rather than weak or soggy; (c) *behaviour of the symptoms* — fluctuating with *mood and stress*, relieved by sighing or belching, whereas this formula's pattern is relieved by *warmth, rest and pressure*. The single sign that decides it is the *wiry pulse against a pale tongue with a greasy coat*: the pulse belongs to the Liver pattern, the tongue to this one. *What to give instead:* 柴胡疏肝散 or 四逆散 where constraint is primary; *柴芍六君子湯 where both are genuinely present*, which is the common real-world answer. *Consequence of the error:* tonifying constraint increases the distension, and the patient reports being worse on the formula — one of the more reliable negative indicators in practice.
- *Food stagnation (食積).* *Why it resembles:* distension, pain, belching, no appetite — nearly the whole picture. *Distinguishing signs:* *aversion* to food rather than reduced appetite; *sour or rotten* belching rather than plain belching; discomfort *worse* with pressure; a thick rather than greasy coat; and no vacuity floor — no fatigue, no weak pulse. *What to give instead:* 保和丸 first, the tonic afterwards if vacuity remains. *Consequence of the error:* worse distension and sour belching.
- *Cold-damp encumbering the Spleen without vacuity (寒濕困脾).* *Why it resembles:* the greasy white coat, the distension, the poor appetite and the cold character are all shared — and this look-alike is close enough that the two formulas built for it (平胃散, 香砂養胃丸) share this one's aromatic pair. *Distinguishing signs:* *heaviness* of the body and head as a leading complaint, a *thick* greasy coat, and a patient who is *robust* — no fatigue out of proportion, no weak pulse, normal complexion. *What to give instead:* 平胃散, or 藿香正氣散 where there is exterior involvement. *Consequence of the error:* the tonics add to the obstruction.
- *Stomach yin vacuity (胃陰虛).* *Why it resembles:* poor appetite with epigastric discomfort and nausea. *Distinguishing signs:* *hunger without desire to eat*, dry retching, dry mouth, and a *red tongue with little or no coat* — the exact inverse of the greasy coat this formula requires. *What to give instead:* a moistening formula. *Consequence:* see above; the drying is now fourfold.
- *Blood stasis in the Stomach channel (胃絡瘀阻) — the look-alike most often missed in chronic cases.* *Why it resembles:* chronic epigastric pain with poor appetite in a tired patient, often with years of history and often *after* a long period of genuine qi vacuity, since stagnant qi eventually stagnates blood. *Distinguishing signs:* the *character of the pain* — fixed in location, stabbing or boring rather than distending, *worse for pressure*, often worse at night and not relieved by belching; plus a *dark or purplish tongue* with distended sublingual veins. *What to give instead:* 丹參飲 or 失笑散 register, or blood-moving drugs added to this formula (see Modifications). *Consequence of the error:* qi-moving alone does not touch fixed stasis, so the patient stalls at partial improvement — and in the biomedical frame this is also where erosive or ulcerative disease and malignancy sit, so persistent fixed pain is a reason to investigate rather than to re-prescribe.
- *The first modern trap: "bloating" is not 氣滯, and functional dyspepsia is not a pattern.* The symptom *bloating* and the biomedical label *functional dyspepsia* are both routinely equated with qi stagnation, and therefore with this formula — it is sold over the counter for precisely that equation. But abdominal distension arises by at least five distinct mechanisms, of which qi-moving addresses *one*: *vacuity* (no power to move anything), *damp* (physical obstruction), *qi stagnation* (constraint), *heat*, and *blood stasis*. *The decisive practical test is directional:* a vacuity-bloating patient feels *worse* after qi-movers and better after rest and warm food, while a stagnation-bloating patient feels better after movement, belching or exercise. *Functional dyspepsia is a label covering several of these at once*, and the formula fits one or two of them.
- *The second modern trap, and it is this formula's own: the parent's clinical evidence does not transfer to it.* 六君子湯, under its Kampo name *rikkunshitō* (TJ-43), has genuine randomized-trial support in functional dyspepsia and chemotherapy-induced anorexia, together with a worked-out ghrelin-signalling mechanism — see `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`. 香砂六君子湯 is treated as the same thing "plus a bit," by practitioners, by suppliers and in review articles. *It is not the same formula.* It contains two additional pharmacologically active drugs, one of them (木香) carrying bioactive sesquiterpene lactones; it has no standardized fixed-composition product equivalent to TJ-43; and *it has not been tested in the trials that are cited for it*. Two consequences, and this book insists on both:
  - *Citing rikkunshitō data for 香砂六君子湯 is the formula-level version of citing single-herb data for a compound formula* — the error this project refuses elsewhere, and refuses here in the other direction.
  - *The reverse inference is also unavailable*: the parent's modest effect sizes are not evidence that this formula underperforms. Nothing has been established either way.
  Separately, *the biomedical floor applies as it does to the parent*: new-onset dyspepsia over 50, dysphagia, weight loss, gastrointestinal bleeding, persistent vomiting, iron-deficiency anaemia or a family history of gastric cancer are *alarm features* requiring investigation before any dyspepsia formula, classical or otherwise. *H. pylori* infection, where present, is treated by eradication therapy; this formula is adjunctive at most.#footnote[Misapplication consequences — the drying effect in yin vacuity, the warming effect in heat patterns, 補其實 and 閉門留寇 — are clinical commonplaces: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. 木香 as 辛溫香燥 with a yin-vacuity caution is the standard _bencao_ caution. The 耗氣 liability of qi-regulating drugs is classical; its framing as the characteristic prescribing error of this family chain is this book's inference and is flagged. Aristolochic acid nephrotoxicity and urothelial carcinogenicity of _Aristolochia_ species, including 青木香 (_A. debilis_), are established in the modern literature and underlie the drug's removal from the _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ — verify against current regulatory listings. The five-mechanism analysis of bloating and the directional test are this book's organization of standard pattern reasoning. The refusal to transfer the rikkunshitō trial literature to this formula is this book's own insistence, consistent with the single-herb-versus-compound distinction maintained in `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`. Dyspepsia alarm features and _H. pylori_ eradication follow standard gastroenterology guidance.]

== Modifications & Derived Formulas

*Named relatives — and note that they are siblings, not descendants*, for the structural reason given under Architecture: this formula is at the end of the addition chain, so the named formulas sharing its aromatic pair are built by *dropping or demoting the tonic core* rather than by adding further drugs.
- *香砂枳術丸 (Xiāng Shā Zhǐ Zhú Wán)* — the aromatics on 枳實 + 白朮; food accumulation with Spleen vacuity, dispersing rather than tonifying.
- *香砂養胃丸 / 香砂養胃湯* — the aromatics with 蒼朮, 厚朴, 香附; cold-damp in the Stomach, drier and much less tonifying.
- *香砂平胃散* — the aromatics on 平胃散; damp obstruction without vacuity.
- *柴芍六君子湯* — *六君子湯 + 柴胡 + 白芍*; the sibling answer where Liver constraint and Spleen vacuity are *both* genuinely present, i.e. where the commonest look-alike above turns out to be a dual pattern. Often given in preference to this formula when the rib-side signs are real.
- *七味白朮散* — *六君子湯* toward 藿香 and 葛根; the paediatric relative for diarrhoea with thirst.

*Common modifications (加減):*
- *+ 乾薑* or *+ 高良薑* — for frank vacuity cold: cold limbs, pain clearly better for warmth, watery stool. This moves the formula toward the 理中丸 register.
- *+ 枳實* or *+ 厚朴* — for stubborn fullness that the aromatics do not shift; used cautiously, since both are downward-breaking and the patient is deficient.
- *+ 黃連 (small dose), ± 吳茱萸* — where mild heat or acid reflux complicates the picture; the 左金丸 pairing. *Frank phlegm-heat contraindicates the base formula*, so this is for a genuinely mixed case, not for heat.
- *+ 蒼朮* — for heavier damp with a thicker coat; more drying and less tonifying than 白朮.
- *+ 柴胡, 白芍* — the 柴芍 register, for a genuine dual Liver-Spleen pattern.
- *+ 丹參, 檀香* (the 丹參飲 pair) or *+ 延胡索, 五靈脂* — where chronic pain has acquired a *stasis* character; addresses the look-alike above when it is a complication rather than a misdiagnosis.
- *+ 焦三仙 (炒山楂, 神曲, 麥芽)* — where food stagnation complicates the vacuity.
- *+ 海螵蛸, 煅瓦楞子* — a *modern* addition for acid symptoms, on an acid-buffering rather than a classical rationale; tagged as modern-tier.
- *黨參 for 人參* — the routine modern substitution, milder and cheaper at larger dose.
- *煨木香 for 生木香* — where loose stools rather than pain dominate; a choice of *drug-form*.
- *姜半夏 for 清半夏* — where vomiting dominates; likewise a drug-form choice.

== Preparation & Administration

*Classical method.* As with its relatives, the Ming and Qing sources give a coarse powder decocted by the dose rather than a large raw-herb brew, with 生薑 added to the water. *The exact wording of whichever edition is taken as the source should be verified* rather than taken from this entry.

*Modern decoction:*
1. Combine 人參 (or 黨參), 白朮, 茯苓, 炙甘草, 陳皮 and the *processed* 半夏 at the doses above, with 生薑 and 大棗. *Hold back 木香 and 砂仁.*
2. Soak in cold water 20–30 minutes; bring to a boil, then simmer 25–30 minutes.
3. *Add 木香 in the last 10–15 minutes* — *木香不宜久煎*, the drug should not be boiled long.
4. *Add 砂仁 in the last 5 minutes (後下)* — its action resides entirely in a volatile oil.
5. Strain; perform a second decoction on the dregs and combine the liquids.
6. Divide into *two to three doses daily*, taken *warm*.

*This is the formula in the family where preparation most changes the result, and the reason is countable.* *Three of its eight named drugs carry volatile-oil actions* — 陳皮, 木香, 砂仁 — and all three are the drugs the formula was built by adding. A decoction boiled hard from the start keeps the tonics intact and drives off much of what distinguishes the prescription from its parents: *the pot reverts, pharmacologically, toward 六君子湯 or even 四君子湯*, which is to say toward the formula for the pattern the patient does *not* have. 參苓白朮散 has the same problem with one drug (see `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`); here it has it with three. *The 後下 handling is therefore constitutive of the formula, not a refinement* — though the step from "volatile oils are lost" to "the clinical effect reverts" is this book's inference from the pharmacy convention, not a demonstrated finding.

*Timing — and the formula differs from a plain tonic in two ways.* Tonics are classically taken *before* food; but where *postprandial distension and pain* are the leading complaint, *30–60 minutes after meals* is the usual clinical adjustment, and where *nausea* dominates, *small frequent warm doses* are preferred. *Warm rather than hot or cold matters*: both extremes provoke vomiting in a patient whose Stomach qi is already counterflowing, and cold decoction aggravates a cold-damp middle. *These adjustments are practical rather than textual.*

*Course.* Weeks; it is a chronic-condition formula and gentle enough for sustained use, with the licorice ceiling (below) the limiting factor. *One course caveat specific to this formula:* because the aromatics are *耗氣*, a long course should be reassessed for the qi-vacuity-without-stagnation failure described under Misapplication — if distension has resolved and fatigue has not, the prescription should step back down the chain rather than continue.

*Modern prepared forms.* *香砂六君丸* is a *Chinese Pharmacopoeia* patent and among the most widely sold over-the-counter dyspepsia preparations in China; granules and tablets are also available, and paediatric presentations exist. Three caveats:
- Concentration ratios vary by manufacturer and are *not interchangeable gram-for-gram* with raw-herb decoction doses.
- *Pill and granule forms cannot reproduce the 後下 handling of 木香 and 砂仁* — a real if unquantified difference, and a larger one here than in any other formula in this book, for the reason given above. Manufacturers who extract the aromatics separately and recombine mitigate this; whether a given product does so is generally not stated on the label.
- *There is no standardized Kampo equivalent*, so the fixed-composition product that made the parent formula's trials possible does not exist for this one.#footnote[The 後下 handling of 砂仁 and the 木香不宜久煎 rule are standard Chinese pharmacy practice; see `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ` for the same constraint on 砂仁 alone. The inference from volatile-oil loss to reversion toward the parent formula's clinical profile is this book's reasoning from the pharmacy convention and is flagged as such rather than asserted as a finding. Post-prandial and small-frequent-warm-dose timing adjustments are ordinary clinical practice, not textual instructions. 香砂六君丸 as a Pharmacopoeia patent: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_, current edition. Granule-to-decoction non-equivalence: manufacturer labelling.]

== Cautions & Contraindications

_Pattern-matching errors are treated above under Misapplication. This section covers pharmacological safety._

*Pattern contraindications (summary; mechanisms above):* Stomach or Spleen yin vacuity and dryness; phlegm-heat, Stomach heat, and Liver constraint with fire; excess damp or food accumulation without vacuity; qi vacuity *without* stagnation; acute exterior illness.

*木香 — substitution and identity, which is the most important safety issue in this formula.* Several drugs carry 木香 in their names and they are *not interchangeable*:
- *木香 (mùxiāng)* — _Aucklandia lappa_ (= _Saussurea costus_, _Dolomiaea costus_), costus root. *The intended drug.* Commercial material is mostly cultivated *雲木香* (Yunnan-grown); imported material is *廣木香*. *A regulatory constraint worth knowing:* _Saussurea costus_ is listed on *CITES Appendix I*, which restricts international trade in wild-sourced material and affects legal availability outside China — *verify current listings and national implementation*, since both change.
- *川木香 (chuān mùxiāng)* — _Dolomiaea souliei_; a *separate Pharmacopoeia drug* with similar but not identical use. Acceptable in its own right, not a silent substitute.
- *土木香 (tǔ mùxiāng)* — _Inula helenium_, elecampane; a different drug with different actions.
- *青木香 (qīng mùxiāng)* — *_Aristolochia debilis_ root. Do not use.* It contains *aristolochic acid*: nephrotoxic, producing irreversible interstitial renal fibrosis, and an established *urothelial carcinogen*, with injury that is *latent and silent* at the time of dosing. It has been removed from the Chinese Pharmacopoeia and is restricted or prohibited in many jurisdictions. *Name similarity to the intended drug is the entire hazard* — specify _Aucklandia_ and reject 青木香 outright. *This is the single caution in this entry that is a matter of permanent injury rather than of pattern mismatch.*
- *木香 itself:* classically *辛溫香燥* and cautioned in *陰虛火旺* (yin vacuity with flaring fire) and *血虛*. No major documented herb-drug interactions, but the data are thin rather than reassuring.

*砂仁* — no significant interaction profile; the practical issue is preparation, not safety. Classically cautioned in *氣虛肺熱* in some _bencao_ sources. Its *安胎* indication means it is classically *used* in pregnancy rather than avoided.

*半夏 — the processing requirement is a safety matter, not a quality preference.* Only processed forms (清半夏, 姜半夏, 法半夏) are given internally; *生半夏 is toxic*, producing immediate oropharyngeal burning, numbness and swelling. Classically *半夏反烏頭* — pinellia counteracts aconite, among the *十八反* — *a canonical prohibition genuinely contested in modern practice*, as recorded in `substances/ban-xia.typ`.

*Pregnancy — a mixed picture that should not be collapsed into a single rule.* The formula contains 砂仁, which is classically a *fetus-calming* drug and is used in pregnancy; 白朮, likewise *安胎*; *半夏*, which is traditionally cautioned yet is prescribed for vomiting of pregnancy in the *金匱要略*'s 小半夏加茯苓湯 and remains in such use in China and Japan (see `substances/ban-xia.typ`); and *木香*, a qi-mover, on which the classical corpus is largely silent and the modern data thin. *The corpus is internally divided and this is a practitioner's decision*, not a rule this entry can state.

*炙甘草 / glycyrrhizin — the limiting factor on duration.* Sustained licorice intake produces *pseudoaldosteronism*: sodium and water retention, *hypokalaemia*, oedema, raised blood pressure. *Caution or avoid* in hypertension, oedema, hypokalaemia, heart failure and renal impairment; *interactions* with diuretics, corticosteroids and digoxin. See `substances/licorice-root.typ`.

*人參* — reduced INR with *warfarin*; additive hypoglycaemia with *antidiabetic medication*; case reports with *MAO inhibitors*; caution in uncontrolled hypertension. Classically *人參反藜蘆* (十八反). See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.

*A caution that does* not *apply, stated to prevent a false alarm.* The interstitial-pneumonitis signal associated with some Kampo preparations attaches principally to formulas containing *黃芩 (Scutellaria)* — notably 小柴胡湯, especially with interferon co-administration. *This formula contains no 黃芩, and is not a Kampo preparation at all*, so the warning is doubly inapplicable. Transferring a warning between formulas because both are Chinese is the same error as transferring efficacy, which this entry refuses above.

*Reference information, not personalized medical advice.* Persistent or fixed epigastric pain, weight loss, vomiting, bleeding or anaemia warrant biomedical investigation rather than a further course of any formula.#footnote[Aristolochic acid nephropathy and urothelial carcinogenicity, and the resulting removal of 青木香 and other _Aristolochia_ drugs from the _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_: established modern literature and regulatory action — verify against current national listings, which differ. The 木香 / 川木香 / 土木香 / 青木香 name-alike set and the 雲木香 / 廣木香 commercial grades follow standard Chinese pharmacognosy. _Saussurea costus_ CITES Appendix I listing: CITES appendices, current edition — verify, as listings and national implementation change. 木香 as 辛溫香燥 with yin-vacuity and blood-vacuity cautions, and the 砂仁 氣虛肺熱 caution, are standard _bencao_ cautions. 半夏 processing, raw-drug toxicity, 半夏反烏頭 and the pregnancy tension: `substances/ban-xia.typ`. Glycyrrhizin pseudoaldosteronism: `substances/licorice-root.typ` and current herb-drug interaction literature. Ginseng interactions: `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`. The Kampo interstitial-pneumonitis signal is associated principally with 黃芩-containing formulas and is noted here only to mark that it does not apply.]

== Modern Pharmacology

*The honest headline: the evidence for this formula is substantially thinner than for its parent, and the parent's evidence does not transfer.* That is an unusual thing to have to say about a *more* elaborated formula, and the reason is structural rather than scientific — 六君子湯 entered the Japanese standardized-extract system as TJ-43 and acquired a fixed composition that can be randomized and placebo-controlled, while 香砂六君子湯 did not. *The comparison is a good illustration of how a formula's evidence base is determined by its manufacturing history as much as by its merits.*

*Clinical literature — what exists, and what it is worth.* There is a substantial *Chinese-language* trial literature, with reported indications including functional dyspepsia (particularly postprandial distress), chronic superficial and atrophic gastritis, *H. pylori*-associated gastritis as an adjunct to eradication therapy, diabetic gastroparesis, irritable bowel syndrome, and chemotherapy-associated gastrointestinal symptoms; several Chinese-journal meta-analyses pool these. *The characteristic limitations of that literature should be stated plainly rather than discounted or ignored:* trials are typically small and single-centre, frequently unblinded, outcomes are symptom scores rather than objective endpoints, comparators are often a prokinetic at a dose chosen by the investigators, allocation concealment is often unreported, and publication bias in the Chinese complementary-medicine literature is well documented. *Verify against current systematic reviews*, and expect the better-conducted reviews to rate the body of evidence as low or very low certainty.

*Preclinical and constituent-level work — and this is single-herb evidence, which is not evidence for the compound.* 木香 yields the sesquiterpene lactones *costunolide* and *dehydrocostus lactone*, with reported anti-inflammatory, gastroprotective and prokinetic activity in animal and cell models. 砂仁's volatile oil, principally *bornyl acetate*, shows gastroprotective and motility effects in similar models. *These are the pharmacologically interesting constituents the formula gains over its parent*, and they are a reason to think the formula is not merely 六君子湯 relabelled — but a plausible mechanism in a constituent is not a demonstrated effect of a decoction, and this book declines to present it as one. The parent formula's *ghrelin-signalling* account, developed in the Kampo literature, presumably applies to the shared six drugs; whether the two additions modify it has not been studied.

*What has not been shown, stated as a list because the gaps are specific:*
- *That this formula outperforms 六君子湯 in the pattern it is supposed to be better for.* This is the central clinical claim of the entire family chain — that each addition earns its place — and it is, for this link, untested in any trial this book is aware of.
- *That the 後下 handling of 木香 and 砂仁 changes clinical outcome.* Pharmacologically plausible, conventionally insisted upon, and unquantified.
- *That pattern-matched prescribing outperforms diagnosis-matched prescribing* — the same unanswered question flagged in the parent entry, and the one this family's literature is best placed to answer and has not asked.#footnote[Chinese-language clinical trial literature and Chinese-journal meta-analyses on 香砂六君子湯 / 香砂六君丸 in functional dyspepsia, chronic gastritis, _H. pylori_ adjunct therapy, diabetic gastroparesis and chemotherapy-associated gastrointestinal symptoms — verify against current systematic reviews and their risk-of-bias assessments; the methodological limitations listed are the standard criticisms of that body of work and are documented in the systematic-review literature. Costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone from _Aucklandia_, and bornyl acetate from _Amomum_, with their preclinical gastroprotective and prokinetic findings: current phytochemical and pharmacological literature, preclinical and single-herb. The rikkunshitō ghrelin mechanism is summarized in `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction.typ`; its extension or non-extension to this composition is unstudied. The explanation of the evidence gap by manufacturing history rather than by merit, and the list of untested claims, are this book's framing.]

